---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../../components/Quiz.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="02-conditionals"
  lessonSlug="03-leap-year"
  hook="The textbook's 'flatten the nested if' lesson with a real-world rule. Three levels deep becomes one Boolean expression."
>
  <h2>The Gregorian rule</h2>
  <blockquote>
    A year is a leap year if it is <strong>divisible by 4</strong>,
    EXCEPT years <strong>divisible by 100</strong> are NOT leap, EXCEPT
    years <strong>divisible by 400</strong> ARE leap.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>The nested approach (don't do this)</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="Works, but hard to read and easy to mess up"
    code={`def is_leap_year(year):
    if year % 4 == 0:
        if year % 100 == 0:
            if year % 400 == 0:
                return True
            else:
                return False
        else:
            return True
    else:
        return False

for y in [2024, 1900, 2000, 2023, 2100]:
    print(y, "->", is_leap_year(y))`}
  />

  <Callout tone="note" title="Source: Think Python §5.6">
    <p>
      <em>"In general, it is a good idea to avoid nested conditionals when you
      can. Logical operators often provide a way to simplify nested conditional
      statements."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The flat approach (do this)</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def is_leap_year(year):
    return (year % 4 == 0 and year % 100 != 0) or (year % 400 == 0)

for y in [2024, 1900, 2000, 2023, 1996, 2100]:
    print(f"is_leap_year({y}) = {is_leap_year(y)}")`}
  />

  <p>Read it aloud: <em>"Divisible by 4 AND not by 100 — OR — divisible by 400."</em></p>

  <h2>Trace 1900 by hand</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>1900 % 4 == 0</code> → <code>True</code></li>
    <li><code>1900 % 100 != 0</code> → <code>False</code></li>
    <li><code>(True and False)</code> → <code>False</code></li>
    <li><code>1900 % 400 == 0</code> → <code>False</code></li>
    <li><code>(False) or (False)</code> → <strong>False</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <Quiz
    question="What about year 2100?"
    choices={[
      { text: "Leap (True)",  explain: "Trap: 2100 is divisible by 100 but NOT by 400 → NOT leap. This is why the rule has the 400 carve-out." },
      { text: "Not leap (False)", correct: true, explain: "Right. 2100 % 100 == 0 kills the first clause; 2100 % 400 != 0 kills the second. Both clauses False → result False." },
      { text: "Depends on calendar", explain: "The Gregorian rule is fully deterministic for any input year." },
    ]}
    hint="Pretend you're Python. Evaluate both clauses separately."
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="A subtle wrong version">
    <p>
      <code>return year % 4 == 0 or year % 400 == 0</code> — missing the
      "century exception". It'd call <code>1900</code> a leap year. The test
      <code>test_century_not_400</code> catches this.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Now you try</h2>
  <p>
    Open <code>03-leap-year.py</code> and implement <code>is_leap_year</code>
    as a single <code>return</code>. The autograder runs 6 tests including all
    three branches of the rule.
  </p>
</Lecture>
